# Apple (Australia) guideline authoring

When writing or updating UI guidance, follow this order:

1. Restate design intent in one sentence.
2. Define foundations and semantic tokens (no raw hex in component sections).
3. Define anatomy, variants, interactions, and every required state.
4. Add accessibility acceptance criteria with pass/fail checks.
5. Add anti-patterns, migration notes, and edge-case handling.
6. End with a QA checklist.

Required document structure: context and goals; tokens; component rules; accessibility; content/tone with examples; anti-patterns; QA checklist.

Non-negotiable rules must use “must”. Recommendations should use “should”. Accessibility rules must be testable.

## Anti-patterns (must not)

- Low-contrast text or hidden focus indicators
- One-off spacing or typography
- Ambiguous labels
- Components without explicit state rules

## QA checklist

- [ ] Tokens only (no stray hex/px)
- [ ] All seven states documented and implemented
- [ ] Keyboard, pointer, and touch specified
- [ ] Long/overflow/empty handled
- [ ] WCAG 2.2 AA checks listed as pass/fail
- [ ] Copy is descriptive and VitaForge-specific
